; ir_rc_rx.loom
;
; Decisions (2026-11-03): no Philips document was reachable, so the
; frame timings cite SB-Projects' descriptions; tolerances are from the
; Vishay TSOP382.. datasheet (doc. 82491 rev. 2.1). For RC-5 the 14 bits
; are reported as received (the address/command split did not fit the
; quarter): the host splits them. The edge-wait dead time (at most 62 us
; at 50 MHz) is below the shortest valid pulse (305 us), so no good frame
; is lost; a too-short pulse is reported only at 50 MHz.
;
; Philips RC-5 and RC-6 mode 0 infrared receiver on IN0, for the output of a
; demodulating receiver module (a Vishay TSOP38236: low while a 36 kHz burst
; is present), delivering frames and errors to the host through OUTQ[0].
; One build takes both: the first mark of a frame tells which.
;
; Written on 2026-11-02, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035). Nothing in Loom was built for
; infrared or for bi-phase codes of this kind; the bit engine's Manchester
; decoder samples a bit cell at a fixed rate and cannot follow RC-6's leader
; and double-length trailer. The program times every mark and space with the
; edge wait (`WAITE ... ANY, T`, SEMANTICS 6.4) against a `SETD` deadline,
; built for the timeouts of PS/2 and I2C, and reads the thread's tick
; counter (`CSRR NOW`) at each confirmed edge. What the firmware does that
; no hardware does: telling the two protocols apart, the pulse windows below,
; the glitch filter, bi-phase decoding by pulse length in both senses, the
; RC-6 leader, start, mode and trailer checks, and the frame report. It needs
; the FIFOs (PUSH) and nothing else: no bit engine, no latch.
;
; Pin: RX = IN0, idle high (the module's output, with its own pull-up).
;
; Result format (FIFO space read, thread 0). Bits 15:14 give the kind:
;   frame:  two words.
;           word 1  bits 15:14 = 01; bit 13 RC-6; bit 12 T, the toggle (RC-5
;                   T, the RC-6 trailer bit); bits 11:0 zero.
;           word 2  the bits as received. RC-6: bits 15:8 the address, 7:0 the
;                   command. RC-5: bits 13:0 S1 S2 T A4..A0 C5..C0 (S1 = 1;
;                   extended RC-5's command bit 6 is the inverse of S2).
;   error:  one word, bits 15:14 = 11. Bits 13:8 h, the halves taken
;           before the fault, or up to the half that broke a pair for a
;           bi-phase fault (RC-5 half-bits from S1's first, 1..28; RC-6
;           units after the leader mark, the leader space's two included,
;           0..46); bit 6 RC-6 (a first mark too long for any leader counts
;           as RC-6, h = 0); bits 2:0 the fault: 1 a pulse outside every
;           window, or a frame running on past its last half; 2 a timeout
;           (the line stopped changing, or a first mark 3.1 ms or longer);
;           3 a bi-phase violation (a bit's two halves equal, or an RC-6
;           leader space or trailer half not two equal units); 4 RC-6 start
;           and mode bits other than 1 000.
; A report is never split: the program blocks on a full OUTQ. The host
; tells a repeat (key held) from a new key press by the toggle, as RC-5 and
; RC-6 intend; the receiver does not keep the last frame.
;
; Tick (set by the host before RUN): one tick is 1/16 of the RC-6 unit, one
; carrier period, 27.78 us.
;   50 MHz: TICK_INT 1388, TICK_FRAC 228 (1388.890625 clocks, +1.1 ppm).
;   1.728 MHz (the RTL tests): TICK_INT 48, TICK_FRAC 0 (exact).
; TICK_INT must be 16 or more (the glitch filter, below, is TICK_INT / 2 - 3
; slots). The program reads TICK_INT once at start.
;
; Protocol and windows. Lengths are counted in ticks between confirmed
; edges. The first mark after an idle line: under 16 ticks (444 us) noise,
; ignored; 16 .. 79 RC-5 (S1's lit half, or with S2's when S2 = 0: 889 or
; 1778 us); 80 .. 111 the RC-6 leader (2.667 ms); 112 or more an error.
; After that each mark and space must be n = 1, 2 or 3 halves (RC-5 half-bit
; q = 32 ticks, 889 us; RC-6 unit q = 16 ticks, 444 us), accepted when its
; length lies in [n - 1/2, n + 1/2) q:
;   RC-6  1 unit  222 .. 667 us   2  667 .. 1111   3  1111 .. 1556 us
;   RC-5  1 half  444 .. 1333 us  2  1333 .. 2222 (3 halves: a violation)
; The decision points are midway between legal lengths, as wide as a
; pulse-length decoder can make them. The receiver module takes the first
; 5 ticks of them: its output pulse is the burst length -5/f0 .. +5/f0
; (139 us at 36 kHz; Vishay TSOP382.., TSOP384.., document 82491 rev. 2.1,
; figure 1 note 3, which names the TSOP38236 "best choice" for RC-5 and
; RC-6), and the count is good to one tick. That leaves +-55 us (12 % of
; a unit) for the transmitter's own error in RC-6 and +-277 us (31 %) in
; RC-5. A frame is over when its last half is in; a last half that is dark
; is taken when the line has been high TMO = 80 ticks (2.22 ms).
;
; Glitch filter. An edge counts only if the new level is still there 2 ticks
; (55.6 us, two carrier periods) later: a shorter pulse of either level is
; ignored and the wait goes on against the same deadline. The module never
; outputs a valid pulse that short: it needs bursts of 10 cycles (278 us)
; and the shortest output pulse in a frame is 444 - 139 = 305 us. A pulse
; longer than the filter but outside its window is an error. Every
; confirmed edge is the filter's delay after the real one, so lengths are
; unchanged.
;
; Halves and bits. Each pulse adds n halves of its level; two halves make a
; bit, which must have a transition in the middle (RC-5: dark-lit is 1;
; RC-6: lit-dark is 1). RC-6 numbers its halves from the leader space:
; halves 1-2 the leader space, 3-10 start and mode (checked as 1 000), 11-14
; the trailer (two equal pairs; T is the first), 15-46 the 16 data bits.
; RC-5 numbers from 19 (S1's dark first half, which an idle line hides) to
; 46, so the RC-6 positions are never met. A stuck mark at the last half is
; caught as a bi-phase violation (h = 28 RC-5, 46 RC-6).
;
; Measurement and dead time. WAITE sees an edge 2 to 5 clocks after it, but
; only an edge that comes while it waits. After each confirmed edge the
; program works for up to 81 slots (the IN to the next WAITE, a 3-half pulse)
; before it waits again: 324 clocks, 6.5 us at 50 MHz but 187.5 us on the
; 1.728 MHz board of the RTL tests. With the filter's 2 ticks that is a dead
; time after every edge (62 us at 50 MHz, 243 us at 1.728 MHz) in which a
; further edge is not seen, so a pulse that short acts as a glitch. Both are
; under the shortest pulse a TSOP38236 gives in a frame (305 us, above),
; so no valid frame is lost; a too-short pulse can only be reported as too
; short (fault 1) when it outlasts the dead time.
;
; Registers: r0 halves to add, then the report; r1 bits 3:0 the shift that
; turns a length into half-halves (3 RC-6, 4 RC-5; bit 0 = RC-6), bit 4
; the level being measured (1 a mark), bit 5 the RC-6 T; r2 the bits; r3 the
; pending first half (2 + level) or 0; r4 halves so far; r5 the glitch
; filter in slots; r6 NOW at the last confirmed edge; r7 lengths and scratch.
;
; Size: 127 words, thread 0's quarter; moved to thread 1 it fits beside
; ir_rc_tx.loom (128 words) in 0x080..0x0FF.

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 0
        .pins   RX = IN0
        .tick   48                      ; TICK_INT 16 or more (header)
        .equ    TMO   = 80              ; ticks: a pulse inside a frame ends by then
        .equ    LTMO  = 112             ; ticks: the longest first mark (RC-6 leader)
        .equ    END   = 46              ; halves: a whole frame (RC-5 counts from 18)
        .equ    E_WIN   = 1             ; fault codes
        .equ    E_TMO   = 2
        .equ    E_BIPH  = 3
        .equ    E_HDR   = 4

start:  CSRR    r5, TICK_INT
        SHRI    r5, 1                   ; P / 2 slots = 2 ticks: the glitch filter,
        SUBI    r5, 3                   ; less the 3 other slots before the IN
        .csr    INGRP, (1 << 5) | 8     ; IN reads RX (IN0, index 8) into bit 0

; Hunt: an idle (high) line, then a confirmed mark. Its length tells the
; protocol, in ticks of 1/16 RC-6 unit (len / 8 is compared):
;   16 .. 47 RC-5, one half-bit;  48 .. 79 RC-5, two;  80 .. 111 RC-6 leader.
hunt:   WAITP   RX, 1
        LDI     r1, 3                   ; s = 3, a space: classify in RC-6 units
        LDI     r4, 0                   ; no half received: h = 0 in an error
h2:     SETD    255                     ; re-armed every 255 ticks
        CALL    wend
        BT      h2                      ; no mark yet
        SETD    LTMO
        CALL    wend                    ; the first mark
        BT      etmo                    ; longer than any leader
        MOV     r0, r7
        SHRI    r0, 3
        CMPI    r0, 2
        BNC     hunt                    ; under 444 us: noise, ignored
        LDI     r2, 0
        CMPI    r0, 10
        BC      rc6
        LDI     r1, 4                   ; RC-5: s = 4, classify in half-bits
        LDI     r4, 19                  ; S1's dark first half, pending
        LDI     r3, 2
        JMP     cls                     ; one lit half, or two (S2 = 0)

; RC-6: the leader space is taken as two dark halves that must be equal,
; as the trailer's are (levels 1 and 2).
rc6:    LDI     r3, 0                   ; nothing pending

; A frame's pulses. Each lasts n = 1, 2 or 3 halves (RC-5 half-bits of
; 32 ticks, RC-6 units of 16): len / q in [n - 1/2, n + 1/2).
body:   CALL    wend
        BT      tmo
cls:    SHR     r7, r7, r1              ; k = len / (q / 2)
        ADDI    r7, 1
        SHRI    r7, 1                   ; n
        MOV     r0, r7
        SUBI    r7, 1
        CMPI    r7, 3
        BC      ewin                    ; n = 0 or n > 3: outside every window

; put: r0 halves of the level of the pulse that has just ended (the
; inverse of r1 bit 4), paired into bits. r3 = 2 + the first half of a pair,
; or 0.
put:    NOT     r7, r1
        SHRI    r7, 4
        ANDI    r7, 1                   ; L: 1 lit
        ADDI    r4, 1
        TEST    r3, r3
        BNZ     pair
        ORI     r7, 2
        MOV     r3, r7                  ; the first half, pending
        JMP     pnext
pair:   XOR     r3, r3, r7              ; 3: the halves differ, 2: equal
        CMPI    r4, 2
        BZ      trl12                   ; RC-6 leader space
        CMPI    r4, 12
        BZ      trl                     ; RC-6 trailer, first half
        CMPI    r4, 14
        BZ      trl12                   ; and second half
        CMPI    r3, 3
        BNZ     ebiph
        XOR     r7, r7, r1
        ANDI    r7, 1                   ; RC-5: the bit is L; RC-6: the first half
        ADD     r2, r2, r2
        OR      r2, r2, r7
        CMPI    r4, 10
        BNZ     pclr
        CMPI    r2, 8                   ; RC-6 start 1, mode 000
        BNZ     ehdr
pclr:   LDI     r3, 0
pnext:  DJNZ    r0, put
        CMPI    r4, END
        BZ      done
        BNC     body                    ; more to come
        JMP     ewin                    ; a frame too long
trl:    SHLI    r7, 5
        OR      r1, r1, r7              ; T, in r1 bit 5
trl12:  CMPI    r3, 2                   ; a trailer half is two equal units
        BNZ     ebiph
        JMP     pclr

; A timeout: the end of a frame whose last half is dark, or an error.
tmo:    CMPI    r4, END - 1
        BNZ     etmo
        ORI     r1, 16                  ; so that put takes a dark half
        LDI     r0, 1
        JMP     put

; A whole frame: report a status word, then the bits as received: RC-6
; the address and the command (r2); RC-5 S1 S2 T A4..A0 C5..C0 (r2 bits 13:0).
; The toggle T is r1 bit 5 for RC-6, r2 bit 11 for RC-5.
done:   MOV     r0, r1
        ANDI    r0, 1                   ; RC-6
        BNZ     d6
        MOV     r7, r2
        SHRI    r7, 11
        JMP     dt
d6:     MOV     r7, r1
        SHRI    r7, 5
dt:     ANDI    r7, 1                   ; T
        ADD     r0, r0, r0
        OR      r0, r0, r7
        ORI     r0, 4                   ; 01, RC-6, T in bits 15:12
        SHLI    r0, 12
        PUSH    r0                      ; blocks while OUTQ is full
        PUSH    r2
        JMP     hunt

; Errors: bits 15:14 = 11, 13:8 the halves received, 6 RC-6, 2:0 the fault.
ebiph:  LDI     r0, E_BIPH
        JMP     err
ehdr:   LDI     r0, E_HDR
        JMP     err
etmo:   LDI     r0, E_TMO
        JMP     err
ewin:   LDI     r0, E_WIN
err:    MOV     r7, r1
        ANDI    r7, 1                   ; RC-6
        BNZ     e6
        SUBI    r4, 18                  ; RC-5 counts its halves from 18
e6:     SHLI    r7, 6
        OR      r0, r0, r7
        LDI     r7, 0xC0
        OR      r4, r4, r7
        SHLI    r4, 8                   ; 11, then h
        OR      r0, r0, r4
        PUSH    r0
        JMP     hunt

; wend: wait for the end of the pulse being measured (r1 bit 4: 1 a mark,
; RX low; 0 a space, RX high) through the glitch filter, or for TD.
; T = 1: TD came first. T = 0: RX changed and the new level held for 2
; ticks (r5 + 3 slots); r7 = the pulse's length in ticks (NOW at this
; confirmation less NOW at the last one, r6, which becomes this one); TD is
; set TMO ticks on and r1 bit 4 flipped. A level that does not hold is a
; glitch, and the wait goes on against the same TD.
wend:   WAITE   RX, ANY, T
        BT      wr
        MOV     r7, r5
wd:     DJNZ    r7, wd
        IN      r7                      ; RX in bit 0
        SHLI    r7, 4
        XOR     r7, r7, r1
        ANDI    r7, 16                  ; 0: RX is the level that ends the pulse
        BNZ     wend                    ; the old level is back: a glitch
        CSRR    r7, NOW
        SUB     r7, r7, r6
        ADD     r6, r6, r7              ; r6 = NOW
        SETD    TMO
        XORI    r1, 16
wr:     RET
